A||||4||William Federle||1984 4 cyl thermostat problem||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||05-03-2000||08:05 PM||||Has anyone besides me had a problem getting a 180 deg stant thermostat to go into the thermostat housing on their 2.5L engine far enough to get the cap on? I did get the cap on over one a few months ago but I have trouble with the cap sealing and it's a real B@#%% to get the cap on and locked. I held the 180 deg therm. up next to a 195 deg one and the bottom of the 195 deg one is smaller in diameter. It also goes into the housing farther so that its top is slightly below the top of the housing. The 180 deg stant thermostat seems to stick up even or slightly above the top of the housing. Or am I just not doing something right?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||wilburt||05-03-2000||08:58 PM||||It sounds from your post that your thermostat is seating fully, so this may not help. My 'stat was hard to get seated, so I lightly coated the 'O' ring with vaseline, and it popped right in. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-04-2000||03:00 PM||||It's definatly not down all the way. It should not interfere with the cap. A little vasaline like wilburt said. lean on it and rock it a bit as you push down. They are pretty snug but they fit properly unless the housing is corroded up bad or damaged.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||05-04-2000||07:34 PM||||Thanks for the advice guys. Saturday is going to be another Fiero fixit day and I'm going to try it then. I appreciate the help and I'll let you know what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||05-06-2000||09:56 PM||||You are right. I used a little grease on the O ring and moved the 180 deg thermostat back and forth a little bit while pushing it in and I got it in below the top of the thermostat housing. Thanks for the tip.<BR>||||reg||1
